INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant, Kaspersky, and WIRED reporting on 2023 3CX compromise", "source_url": "SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-008; SRC-002", "text": "Evidence batch: Mandiant reports the initial compromise of 3CX's network came through malicious software downloaded from Trading Technologies' website: a tampered X_TRADER installer. The attacker used a compiled Fast Reverse Proxy to move laterally in 3CX, harvested credentials, and compromised both Windows and macOS build environments. Windows build environment had TAXHAUL and COLDCAT persistence via IKEEXT DLL search-order hijacking with LocalSystem; macOS build server had POOLRAT using Launch Daemons. Mandiant says trojanized 3CX DesktopApp 18.12.416 and earlier contained SUDDENICON, which obtained C2 URLs from encrypted icon files hosted on GitHub, then downloaded ICONICSTEALER, a browser-information dataminer. Mandiant tracks activity as UNC4736 and assesses with moderate confidence it is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity; it notes POOLRAT overlap with CISA-reported AppleJeus CoinGoTrade and JMT Trading activity, while also noting weak overlap with suspected APT43 clusters UNC3782 and UNC4469. Kaspersky reports infected MSI installers, malicious DLL/shellcode, GitHub-hosted C2 icon URLs, infostealer behavior, and Gopuram as a further/final payload; it attributes the campaign to Lazarus with medium-to-high confidence based on Gopuram coexisting with AppleJeus, cryptocurrency targeting, shared infrastructure, and loader-shellcode similarities, while acknowledging open-source shellcode can be used by other actors. WIRED reports Mandiant's chain: employee PC infected by X_Trader, access spread through 3CX network to development server, 3CX installer corrupted, broad customer distribution; WIRED says actor names Kimsuky, Emerald Sleet, Velvet Chollima, but this is a media naming/attribution presentation and differs from Mandiant's UNC4736 nomenclature. WIRED also reports 3CX software was used by about 600,000 organizations, but precise campaign goals remained uncertain and Kaspersky observed fewer than ten Gopuram-targeted machines."}
